<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bitola</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Monastir</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bitola is a city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelagonia">Pelagonia</a> plain of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a>, near the Greek border and the ruins of the ancient city of Heraclea Lyncestis. Under Ottoman rule, as Monastir, it was a major administrative and military centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>. Serbian forces defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> nearby in the Battle of Monastir in 1912, and it is now the country's second city.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bitola.jpg" alt="Bitola" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tommyy882 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ambientalna_ulica_Marsal_Tito-Bitola_(10).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bitola lies in the southwestern part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a>, in the southern part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelagonia">Pelagonia</a> valley, surrounded by the Baba, Nidže and Kajmakčalan mountain ranges and 14 kilometres north of the Medžitlija-Níki border crossing with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. The Dragor River flows through the city, which stands at 615 metres above sea level at the foot of Baba Mountain. It stands at an important junction connecting the south of the Adriatic region with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean Sea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest organised settlements nearby are early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> tells near the village of Porodin, first inhabited around 6000 BCE. In antiquity the region was Lynkestis, part of Upper Macedonia, and Heraclea Lyncestis was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> by the middle of the 4th century BCE. Slavic tribes settled the area in the 6th and 7th centuries and built a fortress around their settlement. Bitola was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a> from the middle of the 8th to the early 11th centuries and was its last capital, in 1015 to 1018; the Bitola inscription is related to the fortress built in 1015. It then returned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> and was briefly part of the Serbian Empire in the 14th century.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> conquered the city in the mid-1380s, and it was part of the Ottoman Empire as Monastir until 1912. It was the last capital of Ottoman Rumelia, from 1836 to 1867, and the many European consulates earned it the name City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">Consuls</a>. The region was a stronghold of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilinden-preobrazhenie-uprising">Ilinden Uprising</a> of 1903, planned in Smilevo in May. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-balkan-war">First Balkan War</a> the Battle of Monastir, from 16 to 19 November 1912, led to Serbian occupation, and the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a> in 1913 ceded Monastir to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, when its official name became Bitola.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> the Germans took control on 9 April 1941, and on 11 March 1943 Bulgarian forces deported the vast majority of its Jewish community, 3,276 people, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treblinka-extermination-camp">Treblinka extermination camp</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Bitola is an administrative, cultural, industrial, commercial and educational centre of southwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a>. Its population was historically diverse: about 37,500 at the end of the 19th century, including Aromanians, a Muslim population, a Jewish community of Sephardic origin and Slavic speakers divided between the Bulgarian Exarchate and the Greek Patriarchate.</p><p>The ruins of Heraclea Lyncestis lie 2 kilometres from the centre. Ottoman monuments include the covered bazaar, the Gazi Hajdar Kadi Mosque of the early 1560s, ascribed to the architect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mimar-sinan">Mimar Sinan</a>, and the Deboj Bath. The Church of Saint Demetrius was built in 1830 with contributions from local merchants and craftsmen. The St. Clement of Ohrid University of Bitola was founded in 1979.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bitola” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Berziti were among the Slavic groups that settled in Macedonia in the 7th century, in the region around Ohrid, Prilep and <b>Bitola</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/berziti">Berziti</a></li></ul></section>
